+### 2.3 Abstract（证据约束修订稿）
+
+> Effective transport models for nanoimprint lithography replace unresolved grooves with an anisotropic permeability closure. We assess the arithmetic–harmonic local-cubic-law closure using steady, fully filled Newtonian flow in ideal rectangular grooves. At a period of 240 nm and width of 120 nm, the map contains 18 sampled geometries and 36 principal-direction calculations, spanning gaps of 25–125 nm and depths of 25–120 nm without covering every combination. The closure overestimates the computed transport coefficients by 10.30–204.85% along the grooves and 8.11–54.19% across them. At the reference geometry, it predicts an anisotropy ratio of 3.70 rather than 2.21, producing a 9.20° difference in the flux-angle prediction for 45° forcing. Geometry-specific refinement and internal consistency checks support the comparisons but do not establish a full-map uncertainty bound. The adopted correction uses groove aspect ratio and coefficients fitted on nine geometries. With published rounded coefficients, maximum absolute residuals are 10.505% over the 18 parallel samples and 6.921% over the 15 perpendicular samples with gaps of at least 50 nm. A perpendicular sample at a 25 nm gap retains a −18.705% residual. Model selection followed access to extension results; these evaluations are retrospective, not independent final prediction tests or continuous-domain guarantees. Side-wall shear and step-region redistribution remain candidate mechanisms rather than quantitatively separated contributions. The results concern fixed-gap, no-slip transport and do not validate dynamic filling or universal transfer to real resists.
